  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This Capsaspora owczarzaki genome dataset shows early but tangible scholarly reuse: it has been cited once, downloaded 5 times, and viewed 19 times. The record also captures two documented downstream reuse signals, indicating that the dataset is being used in ongoing research despite a still-small impact footprint.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Genome - Capsaspora owczarzaki (v3) is a green open-access dataset deposited in Figshare in 2016 by Multicellgenome Lab. It is a genome assembly and annotation resource for Capsaspora owczarzaki, a close unicellular relative of metazoans, and its subject profile centers on genomics, biology, genetics, and computational biology.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of impact is scholarly uptake. OpenAlex records one citation, and the downstream citing literature places the dataset in a 2025 eLife article on G-protein-coupled receptor diversity and evolution in the closest living relatives of metazoa. Crossref data-citation tracking likewise registers two dataset citation events, showing that the resource is being referenced explicitly as a dataset in the scholarly record.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Reuse signals extend beyond formal citation. The reuse graph captures two documented downstream reuses, including an article-level reuse and a biomedical article mention indexed by Europe PMC. Taken together, these signals suggest that the dataset is serving as a reusable reference resource for work on protist biology and the evolution of multicellularity.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage is modest but visible, with 19 recorded views and 5 downloads. Public discourse evidence is not available from the archive, so the record supports a cautious interpretation: the dataset has clear early scholarly utility, but the observable impact remains concentrated in a small number of research uses rather than broad public attention.</p>",
